--  Acting on whether something worked.
--
--  A command is a procedure: `run ("false");` produces no value. Without a way
--  to ask afterwards, a program could run something and never learn whether it
--  worked -- which is most of what a shell script does. `Status` is that ask,
--  and it is `$?` under an Ada name.
--
--  The number is the one exit-status model `Adash.Execution` documents and the
--  shell itself exits with: 0 for success, what an external program chose,
--  126 for something found and not executable, 127 for something not found,
--  128 + n for a program a signal killed. A script forwarding a status does not
--  have to translate it.

--  A program that succeeds and a program that fails, named the way this host
--  names them.
--
--  There is no program every host has: `true` and `false` are POSIX, and where
--  they are missing the interpreter is asked to exit with a number instead.
--  Which is a real lesson about a shell that runs programs -- what a program is
--  called, and how its arguments are shaped, is the host's business and not the
--  language's -- and the reason the shell answers `Is_Executable` at all.
--
--  Asked of the interpreter every POSIX host keeps in the same place, because
--  the programs themselves are not kept in one: `/bin/true` on one host is
--  `/usr/bin/true` on another, and the names below find whichever it is.
Posix : Boolean := Is_Executable ("/bin/sh");

procedure Run_Something (Working : Boolean) is
begin
   if Posix then
      run (if Working then "true" else "false");
   else
      run ("cmd", "/c", if Working then "exit 0" else "exit 1");
   end if;
end Run_Something;

Run_Something (True);
put_line (Status);

Run_Something (False);
put_line (Status);

--  Which makes a subprogram that answers the question directly, in the
--  language rather than by a convention of the shell.
function Ran (Working : Boolean) return Boolean is
begin
   Run_Something (Working);
   return Status = 0;
end Ran;

if Ran (True) then
   put_line ("that one worked");
end if;

if not Ran (False) then
   put_line ("that one did not");
end if;

--  It is an ordinary Integer: it compares, it goes into a variable, and it does
--  arithmetic.
Last : Integer := Status;

put_line (f"the last one exited {Last'Image}");
